WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.168 --> 00:00:02.640
I hear from lots of
people every day

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:07.200
who are concerned about how
their diet is affecting their health.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.920
They need answers
based on facts.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:10.840
In other words,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.800
in the peer-reviewed
medical literature,

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.640
and that is what
I’m here for.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.920
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.800
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.040
If you think the claim of
‘younger looking skin overnight’

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.720
sounds a bit too good to be true,
you may be right.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.440
Today is the first in our two-part series
on the efficacy of anti-aging creams.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.280
What is the truth about
over-the-counter “anti-aging” products,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.000
which constitute
a billion-dollar industry.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.080
There is a “psychological effect
from spending more,”

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.000
wrote a team of dermatologists,

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.640
but “don’t be seduced by fancy
packaging and high prices.”

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.400
Many products advertise dramatic results
that are frequently exaggerated

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.280
and misleading and are rarely
scientifically supported.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.480
An independent product testing
institute questioned the efficacy

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.720
of anti-aging creams generally,

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.000
find that beneficial effects

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.720
could only be picked up
using sensitive instruments

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.200
without becoming clinically
or noticeably detectable.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.960
They suggest these products may not work
any better than typical moisturizers.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.880
Cross-sectional studies
of Chinese and British women found

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.400
that those who regularly
used facial moisturizers

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.880
were guessed to be about
two years younger

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.600
than those the same age who didn’t.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.320
However, a third, larger Dutch study
did not, and regardless,

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.320
snapshot-in-time studies can
never establish cause-and-effect.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.040
Studies on moisturizers are limited.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.280
But they can improve
the appearance of dry skin,

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.040
which can otherwise look discolored,
flaky, and rough.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.000
Moisturizers can hydrate the skin
and may reduce the appearance of fine lines

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.480
by 15 to 20%, called “the oldest trick
[in] the cosmetic industry,”

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.640
but may not do anything
to treat the underlying cause.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:57.480
Whether facial foundation,
or night cream, or anti-aging serum,

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.080
the formulations of most skin products
are basically a moisturizer

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.040
combined with purported active
ingredients for marketing appeal.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.760
Which ingredients are actually
active anti-aging agents?

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.080
I’ll give you hint:

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:15.880
Up to 90% of the visible aging of
someone’s face is due to sun exposure.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:17.320
From an anti-aging standpoint,

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:22.920
the most biologically active ingredient
in skin products is sunscreen.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.160
Considered the most important thing
to maintaining youthful skin

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:27.880
is the daily application of sunscreen

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.240
and other protective measures
like wearing a hat.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.720
All other things you can do
for your skin pale in comparison,

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.320
especially for those
with pale skin.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.280
To prevent skin cancer,
the American Academy of Dermatology

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.280
recommends sunscreen
with an SPF of 30 or higher,

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.400
but an SPF of 15
can prevent skin aging.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.320
How do we know?
Because it’s been put to the test.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:54.600
Nine hundred adults were randomized
to years of daily sunscreen use

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.840
or to continue with their own
discretionary use.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.440
(It was considered unethical
to withhold protection

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.840
by giving people placebo sunscreen.)

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.400
In the end, 77% in the recommended
daily sunscreen group

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.800
were applying sunscreen at least
three to four days per week

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:13.880
compared with 33% in the
discretionary use group.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:17.680
Would that be enough
of a difference to make a difference?

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.560
Yes, there was significantly less skin
aging in the instructed daily use group.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.240
In fact, they suffered no
detectable increase in skin aging

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.680
over the 4.5-year study.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:29.920
The researchers concluded,

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.800
“Daily sunscreen use protects
against skin aging.”

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.600
Although sunscreens are primarily
intended to prevent further facial aging

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.200
rather than reverse
photodamage already done,

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.720
some in the daily sunscreen use group
did show an improvement in skin texture.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.160
The results are all the more striking
given the control group was told

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:52.800
to continue to use sunscreen and hats
whenever they thought necessary,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.720
suggesting people are poor judges
or planners for excess UV exposure

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.880
when left to their own devices.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.400
So, a daily facial moisturizer
with an SPF 15 in it

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:06.760
is recommended, even if it’s cloudy
or raining outside.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:09.960
Considered the “gold standard”
for anti-aging skin care:

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:14.680
“daily use of sunscreens in the
daytime and retinoids at night.”

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:15.920
Retinoids?

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.911
I’ll cover them next.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.320
Sunscreens are intended to prevent
further facial photoaging

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:26.320
but can’t dramatically reverse
damage presently visible,

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.280
whereas topical retinoids
are the most important drug class

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.080
to reverse the signs of aging.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.440
Also known as all-trans
retinoic acid, tretinoin

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:38.360
(sold under a variety
of brands including Retin-A)

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:41.560
is a prescription-only
topical form of vitamin A

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:44.920
that can visibly improve mild
to moderate photodamage,

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.720
including fine and coarse wrinkles,
freckles, other pigmentation,

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:52.520
and improve overall skin texture
after months of daily use.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.680
This is accompanied by
a restoration of collagen formation.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.160
Skin biopsies taken before
and after 10 to 12 months

00:04:58.160 --> 00:04:59.880
of tretinoin versus placebo

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.520
found that while skin collagen formation
dropped 14% in the control group,

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:08.320
it rose 80% in the retinoic acid group.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.960
The downsides are the side-effects,
so common they have their own name—

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:15.240
“retinoid reaction”—plaguing
a high proportion of patients,

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.800
including redness, stinging,
burning, itching, and peeling.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.480
Ironically, the constant irritation
may be a causal mechanism,

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:24.920
constantly forcing the skin to renew.

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:27.480
The adverse effects do
tend to improve over time

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.960
after a peak after about
two weeks of daily use.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:33.320
And you can minimize the irritation
by starting at a lower concentration

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:36.880
(for example .025% instead of 0.1%)

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:41.160
and slowly ramping up from applying
at first twice a week for a few weeks,

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.400
then to every other night
for a few more weeks

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:47.400
before finally advancing
to nightly application as tolerated.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.120
Given that a small amount
is systemically absorbed,

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:53.640
out of an abundance of caution
women of child-bearing capacity

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.760
may want steer clear,
given the severe birth defects

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:59.800
caused by a similar drug,
isotretinoin

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:03.160
(sold formerly
as the acne drug Accutane).

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:07.240
There are gentler, less potent,
over-the-counter topical retinoids:

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:09.520
retinyl, retinol, and retinal.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:11.760
There’s retinyl acetate,
retinyl palmitate,

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:14.680
retinyl propionate, retinol,
and retinaldehyde.

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:16.000
Once absorbed into your skin,

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.760
your body can convert them
into small amounts of retinoic acid.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:24.240
However, data is limited on these
nonprescription retinoids.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.720
The results of clinical trials
on the retinyl esters

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:29.480
have been routinely disappointing.

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:32.720
Even 48 weeks of daily use
was found to be ineffective

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:35.840
for reducing wrinkles
compared to placebo.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:39.760
Retinaldehyde and retinol,
however, hold some promise.

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:41.600
There was a 48-week
head-to-head trial

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:45.120
of retinaldehyde vs.
retinoic acid vs. placebo.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:48.800
Now retinoids can be so irritating
that it’s hard to blind participants

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:50.640
as to whether or not
they’re in the control group;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.040
so, objective measures that may be
less susceptible to placebo effects

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:55.640
are especially important.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:58.000
So, what they did
is make silicone molds

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.040
of the crow’s feet wrinkles
around the eyes of the participants

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:04.200
before and after being randomized
to one of those three groups,

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.520
and then analyzed scans
of the molds

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.760
using high-resolution
digital image processing.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:11.960
The retinaldehyde group
showed a similar reduction

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:16.000
in fine lines and wrinkles as the
retinoic acid group with less irritation.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.480
Only 23% of the retinaldehyde group
experienced skin irritation,

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:25.760
compared to 71% in the retinoic acid group,&nbsp;
versus 4% in the placebo cream group.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:28.080
Of all the nonprescription
retinoid options,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.880
retinol may be the preferred choice, though.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.720
It causes even less irritation
than retinaldehyde

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:35.920
(insignificantly different than placebo),

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:38.080
and a randomized double-blind,
placebo-controlled trial

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.440
found that 52 weeks of use
improved crow’s feet fine lines

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:47.280
by 44% and mottled pigmentation by 84%.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:53.600
But tretinoin—retinoic acid—has by far
the most robust track record of efficacy;

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:57.000
so, why not just ask
your doctor for a prescription?

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.520
Because long-term topical tretinoin
use may increase your risk

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:05.600
of an even more
stinging side-effect: death.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:08.680
Topical Tretinoin Therapy
and All-Cause Mortality:

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:12.400
The Veterans Affairs Topical
Tretinoin Chemoprevention trial

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:15.320
was to be a six-year randomized
controlled trial to see

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.840
if it could help
prevent skin cancer.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:21.240
But the trial had to be stopped early
because significantly more people

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:26.040
were dying in the retinoic acid group
than in the placebo-cream group.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:27.720
At the time the study was halted,

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:30.600
19% of the subjects
in the tretinoin group had died,

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:34.000
compared to 14% in the placebo group.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:38.160
Between 1 to 8%
of topically applied retinoic acid

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.040
is absorbed into the bloodstream.

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:42.480
Could it be killing people?

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.720
We don’t know if the increase
in deaths was a statistical fluke

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:49.560
that happened by chance
or a real biological effect.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:53.280
The probability that we’d see
such a discrepancy just by chance

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:55.720
is about 1 in a 100.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:01.806
Tretinoin continues to be banned
in Europe for cosmetic purposes.

